dle> that , \" Mr. Marous <ei22 said> . GNP is the total value of the nation 's output of goods and services . A bright spot is the company 's power-generation business , which is <ei23 experiencing> a surge of <ei24 growth> for the first time in years . Mr. Marous <ei26 said> the business will <ei27 achieve> higher sales this year than the company 's target goal of 8.5 % . While Westinghouse has n't <ei29 had> a nuclear power plant order from a U.S. utility in about a decade , excess capacity is <ei31 beginning> to <ei32 shrink> . Mr. Lego <ei33 said> the company <ei34 foresees> the need for a major <ei35 boost> in new-generation capability throughout the 1990s . Westinghouse also is well <ei37 positioned> to <ei38 sell> steam turbine and gas turbine plants to independent power producers . The company 's ability to respond to energy needs world-wide will be <ei39 enhanced> through a recently announced <ei41 venture> with Mitsubishi Heavy Industries , Mr. Lego <ei42 said> . He <ei43 said> the independent power segment could <ei44 grow> to <ei45 provide> as much as 50 % of near-term generation capacity , <ei46 adding> : \" We <ei47 expect> to <ei48 supply> a significant share of this market . \" Westinghouse also <ei49 expects> its international sales to soon <ei51 grow> to 25 % of total corporate sales from 20 % last year . The company is <ei53 negotiating> with the Soviets to <ei54 build> a Thermo King truck-refrigeration plant that would <ei55 produce> about 10,000 units annually . Mr. Marous <ei56 said> Westinghouse would <ei57 own> 70 % of the facility . The <ei58 deal> , which will <ei59 involve> an initial $ 20 million <ei60 investment> , was <ei61 struck> with a <ei62 handshake> , he <ei63 added> . Company officials also <ei64 said> that any gain from the <ei65 sale> of Westinghouse 's 55 % stake in its transmission and distribution venture with the Swiss firm of Asea Brown Boveri will be <ei66 offset> by a restructuring charge in the fourth quarter . The exe